## Description

With replicas=1, maxSurge=1, maxUnavailable=0, partition=0 and RecreatePod, we observed old router-0 and surge router-1 disappearing close together during a v0.8.0 rollout, before the replacement was available. v0.7.0 had preserved the surge until the replacement became Ready.

A fake-client regression confirms that getExpectOwnedPods() accepts a same-name Pod without checking its controller owner UID or deletion timestamp. Consequently, a new RoleInstance UID can report AllPodsReady=True using an old UID's Pod, or a terminating Pod whose Ready condition is still True. The full rollout/GC race is a proposed explanation for the outage and has not yet been reproduced end-to-end in a cluster.

## Code evidence

Checked main at 95440e391f242501215308922522895a05f3006b:
https://github.com/sgl-project/rbg/blob/95440e391f242501215308922522895a05f3006b/pkg/reconciler/roleinstance/instance_status.go#L245

The regular reconciliation path filters Pods by owner UID, but readiness independently gets them by name. Commit 157865d646452accddc974f052c8831bdb661e48 (#393) makes RecreatePod delete/recreate RoleInstances rather than first attempting an in-place instance update, exposing this same-name/different-UID window. Older versions can also be affected when they take the delete/recreate path.

## Possible outage sequence

1. New surge router-1 becomes Ready.
2. Old RoleInstance router-0 (UID A) is deleted, with its Pod temporarily remaining pending asynchronous GC.
3. Replacement RoleInstance router-0 (UID B, target revision) is created, but has no Pod yet.
4. Readiness reads the old same-name Pod and can mark UID B ready once its other readiness gates are satisfied.
5. allBaseAtUpdateRevHealthy()/topology accepts this false readiness and permits surge reclamation.
6. Old router-0 Pod GC overlaps router-1 deletion, leaving no available router.

## Expected behavior and proposed fix

Keep the surge until the Pod controlled by the replacement RoleInstance UID is actually available. In getExpectOwnedPods(), reject Pods unless metav1.IsControlledBy(pod, instance) is true and pod.DeletionTimestamp is nil. Use the existing error-to-NotReady path. Both negative regressions below pass with these checks; the valid owner/Ready case and the roleinstance package tests also pass. A cluster regression with delayed GC is still needed.

## Environment

- Observed versions: v0.7.0 / v0.8.0; deployed image digest, build commit and Kubernetes version have not been independently verified.
- Code/test verification: main commit above; Go 1.25.0, darwin/arm64, vendored dependencies, controller-runtime fake client.
- This is a Go controller bug; no SGLang model/GPU/Python environment is needed for the minimal reproduction.

## Reproduction

Save this as `pkg/reconciler/roleinstance/router_owner_regression_test.go` and run:

```sh
go test -mod=vendor ./pkg/reconciler/roleinstance -run '^TestRouterRecreatedInstanceMustNotUseOldPodReady$' -count=1
```

```go
package roleinstance

import (
 "testing"
 corev1 "k8s.io/api/core/v1"
 metav1 "k8s.io/apimachinery/pkg/apis/meta/v1"
 "k8s.io/apimachinery/pkg/runtime"
 "k8s.io/utils/ptr"
 "sigs.k8s.io/controller-runtime/pkg/client/fake"
 workloads "sigs.k8s.io/rbgs/api/workloads/v1alpha2"
)

func TestRouterRecreatedInstanceMustNotUseOldPodReady(t *testing.T) {
 scheme := runtime.NewScheme()
 _ = corev1.AddToScheme(scheme)
 instance := &workloads.RoleInstance{
  ObjectMeta: metav1.ObjectMeta{Name: "router-0", Namespace: "default", UID: "new-instance"},
  Spec: workloads.RoleInstanceSpec{Components: []workloads.RoleInstanceComponent{
   {Name: "router", Size: ptr.To(int32(1))},
  }},
 }
 pod := &corev1.Pod{
  ObjectMeta: metav1.ObjectMeta{
   Name: "router-0-router-0", Namespace: "default",
   OwnerReferences: []metav1.OwnerReference{{
    APIVersion: "workloads.x-k8s.io/v1alpha2", Kind: "RoleInstance",
    Name: "router-0", UID: "old-instance", Controller: ptr.To(true),
   }},
  },
  Status: corev1.PodStatus{Phase: corev1.PodRunning, Conditions: []corev1.PodCondition{
   {Type: corev1.PodReady, Status: corev1.ConditionTrue},
  }},
 }
 for _, tc := range []struct {
  name string
  oldOwner bool
  terminating bool
  wantReady bool
 }{
  {"old owner UID", true, false, false},
  {"current owner terminating", false, true, false},
  {"current owner ready", false, false, true},
 } {
  t.Run(tc.name, func(t *testing.T) {
   candidate := pod.DeepCopy()
   if !tc.oldOwner { candidate.OwnerReferences[0].UID = instance.UID }
   if tc.terminating {
    now := metav1.Now()
    candidate.DeletionTimestamp = &now
    candidate.Finalizers = []string{"test-hold"}
   }
   updater := &realStatusUpdater{
    Client: fake.NewClientBuilder().WithScheme(scheme).WithObjects(candidate).Build(),
   }
   condition := updater.getAllReadyCondition(instance)
   if got := condition.Status == corev1.ConditionTrue; got != tc.wantReady {
    t.Fatalf("AllPodsReady=%v, want %v", got, tc.wantReady)
   }
  })
 }
}
```

Actual: the old-owner and terminating-Pod subtests fail with `AllPodsReady=true, want false`; the valid current-owner Ready case passes.

Full rollout verification still needed: delay old base Pod GC during a single-replica RecreatePod rollout and assert that the surge is retained until the Pod owned by the replacement RoleInstance UID is actually available. This cluster-level test has not yet been run.
